  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote the tower-stacking game in `src/main.ts`. It typechecks, but I couldn't run `vite build` or open it in a browser, so I haven't seen it render or play.\n\n- **Scene:** I used the `outdoor-day` look, because the docs say a scene without a look renders flat. The slabs and falling pieces are boxes created up front as 26 slabs (base plus 25) and 25 debris pieces. They are moved, scaled, hidden and shown at runtime. A tall pillar sits under the base so the tower doesn't float. I couldn't check whether the look's own ground overlaps it.\n- **Camera:** a follow camera tracks a tiny hidden node that eases upward as the tower grows. I used this because `app.camera` looked flag-gated and I couldn't confirm it exists.\n- **Rules:**\n  - The slab slides along x. Space or a click on the canvas drops it.\n  - Only the overlapping part stays. The overhang spawns a piece that falls and spins away.\n  - A drop within 0.1 units keeps the full width and adds +1 bonus on top of the +1 for stacking.\n  - A complete miss makes the whole slab fall and sets `lost`.\n  - Reaching 25 stacked slabs sets `won`.\n  - Speed rises every 3 levels, and slab colours shift in hue per level.\n- **Restart and HUD:** R restarts at any time, and a click on the canvas restarts after the game ends. A DOM HUD below the canvas shows score, best score for the page session and status. `window.__TASK_STATE__` holds `{ status, score, height }` and is updated on every change.\n- **Rendering:** my own `requestAnimationFrame` loop drives the game and sets `__TASK_READY__` after the second frame. The game still runs and the state still updates if the 3D mount fails.\n\n`height` counts stacked slabs and does not count the base, so a fresh game has `height: 0` and `score: 0`. The task text was ambiguous here, so say if you want the base counted.\n\n",
